#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-room — does the box have room for one more headless worker, and a queue for the launch when it has not.

  cc-room check                     exit 0 there is room, on this host or the peer · 1 there is not, and the one
                                    line on stdout says why
  cc-room admit <repo/track> [--log FILE] [--resume ENTRY] -- CMD…
                                    wait until there is room and this launch is the oldest one waiting. Room on
                                    this host: take a slot, then exec CMD — the same pid, so the slot is held
                                    exactly as long as CMD runs and frees itself when it exits. Room on the peer
                                    only: hand the launch to `cc-peer launch` and exit with it. Nobody retypes a
                                    queued launch.
  cc-room cancel <repo/track>       drop that launch from the queue: its waiting admit finds the tombstone on its next
                                    poll and exits 0 without running CMD. Nothing is killed. exit 1 when none is queued
  cc-room bump <repo/track>         move that launch to the head of the queue (PRIORITY, below). exit 1 when none is
                                    queued
  cc-room now <repo/track>          start that launch on its waiter's next poll, room or not (PRIORITY). exit 1 when
                                    none is queued
  cc-room resume                    relaunch each launch a restart left behind (LOST, below) in a tmux window of its
                                    own, in its old place in the queue. cc-reconcile's tick runs it every 20 min
  cc-room heavy [--name L] [--log FILE] -- CMD
                                    a CPU-heavy tool run (annealing, SPICE, routing): wait for one of CC_HEAVY_CAP heavy
                                    slots, then exec CMD in a user scope that competes as one block (HEAVY, below)
  cc-room status                    the slots held, the launches queued and the ones lost in a restart
  cc-room queued                    one `<repo/track>` line per launch that will start by itself, a lost one
                                    `resume` restarts included (not one stamped `placing`):
                                    cc-reconcile's wake leaves these rows out, since their board status stays
                                    `queued` while they wait and the seat has nothing to start
  cc-room selfcheck

ROOM MEANS ALL THREE; the first two are cc-config keys (the environment wins, as it does for every key):
  CC_WORKER_CAP (12)          fewer than this many worker loops hold a slot;
  CC_WORKER_PACE_MARGIN (10)  the account's five-hour usage is not ahead of the window's elapsed time by more than
                              this many points: used% <= elapsed% + margin. It reads cc-usage-poll's live reading
                              (~/.cc/state/usage/now.json, CC_USAGE_DIR) while it is under 3 min old, else the
                              freshest statusline file (~/.cc/state/statusline/*.json, CC_STATUSLINE_DIR), either
                              only while its window has not reset yet.
                              No such file, or a window already over, is room — a reading nobody has is never
                              the reason a worker waits.
  the box is not busy       the lander's own overloaded() (core/lander/admission.py): memory or cpu pressure, or a
                              1-minute load average at LANDER_LOAD_FACTOR (2) times the cores or more. It is the
                              same call the lander serializes on, so while the box is overloaded new workers wait
                              and the landing queue gets the room the load frees as it drains. A hold by the
                              load average alone still lets one through now and then (TRICKLE, below).
Nothing here reads the weekly limit. The one thing it ever ends is a tmux window `resume` itself opened, and only
while every process in it is the `cc-room admit --resume` waiter that window was opened for, the shell line around
it, or the idle shell it leaves behind: a launch that never started. Anything else in that window (the launch it
went on to run, or a person's command) means the window is left alone, and so is a window it cannot read.

TWO HOSTS (owner, 2026-10-12: "ideally full ram and cpu usage on both boxes"). With a peer configured (cc-peer:
~/.cc/peer), a launch this host has no room for goes to the peer when the peer has room by its own count — its own
cap, load and memory, asked over the launch key (`cc-peer room`) — and otherwise waits here as before. This host
first, then the peer, then the queue. The five-hour pace is the account's, the same on both, so a launch held by it
waits and the peer is not asked. Only a cc-loop launch can move; a member workspace's stays on its host. A peer that
said no, did not answer or did not take a launch is not asked again for PEER_REST seconds. The peer is asked with the
room lock released, so a slow one holds up no other admit, heavy run, cancel or resume. A worker on the peer holds a
slot there, not here.

WHY (2026-09-27): about 30 workers started inside an hour; load sat at 130-185 on 6 cores with swap full, the OOM
killer ran 7 times and timed units failed one after another, while the five-hour window ran ahead of its clock.

Only `cc <repo> <track> --go` calls `admit` (it wraps the loop's tmux window in it). Interactive seats and landings
never pass through here, so they are never counted and never capped. A slot is a file under ~/.cc/state/room/slots
(CC_ROOM_DIR) naming a pid and its start time; a waiting launch is a file under queue/. A slot is cleared as soon
as its process is gone, and a queue entry is too unless a restart took it (LOST, below). A loop started before this tool existed holds no slot, so it is not counted.
A paused project never gets here (`cc` refuses --go first), and a launch that waited through a pause is still
refused by cc-loop's own `cc-pause is` check the moment it starts.

LOST (2026-10-01: three owner-asked workers queued 2.5 h died with the 18:32Z tmux-main restart and nothing brought
them back, because they had never started). A queue entry is JSON and carries what it takes to start the launch
again: CMD, its cwd and log, the environment `cc … --go` carries into the window (RELAUNCH_ENV, an allow-list: no
token rides along), its tmux session and window, and the tmux server it
waits in, with its socket (the first field of $TMUX), so `resume` reaches that server and not the default one. A
waiter that is gone while that server is ALIVE was ended on purpose (its window killed) — or the server is still on
its way down in a restart, which ends the waiter first. So the entry waits CC_ROOM_GRACE seconds (300) out of the
FIFO, the first sighting stamped in it, and goes only if the server is still up then. One whose server is gone too
died with a restart: the entry stays, out of the FIFO so it holds nobody
up, until `resume` starts `admit --resume <entry>` in a new window of the same name — same entry, same place, same
queued-since — or a fresh `cc … --go` of the same track adopts it. A window of that name already open means someone
started it by hand, and the entry goes instead. A window `resume` itself opened whose admit never wrote the entry is
looked at first: holding only that waiter or its idle shell, it is closed and opened again; running anything else,
the launch went ahead on its own, so the entry goes and the window stays; unreadable, both stay and the log says so.
An `admit --resume` that fails open drops its entry before it runs CMD, so `resume` has nothing left to act on.
`admit --resume` of an entry whose waiter is alive starts nothing: someone adopted it first. The saved environment
goes to the window with `tmux new-window -e`, never into its command line. An entry nothing resumes in 7 days goes.
`resume` does its tmux calls outside the room lock, one `resume` at a time, and re-reads an entry under the lock
before it changes it, so a cancel or an adoption in between is never written over.

PLACING (landing's security read of #1159). Before a waiter calls `cc-peer launch` it stamps its entry `placing`
under the lock, and a peer that did not take the launch clears the stamp. A waiter killed between the two leaves a
lost entry `resume` never relaunches blind, since the peer may already run it: when cc-peer's placed/REPO/TRACK
record is as new as the stamp, the launch runs there and the entry goes; otherwise the entry stays, the log says so
once and status names it, until a person checks the peer and cancels it or launches it again (a fresh `cc … --go`
adopts it and drops the stamp).

NOTICE (2026-10-01: a light website worker sat 3 h behind "the box is busy" while the owner chased it twice). A
launch queued CC_ROOM_NOTICE_AFTER seconds (cc-config, 3600) tells the seat that dispatched it, once: `cc-broker
inject --source room` to `<repo>@<alias>` when an orch dispatched it (CC_SLACK_ALIAS, which `cc … --go` carries into
the window), else `<repo>`; a broker that does not take it files a `cc-notify --ask` instead. The line says why it
waits and how to cancel it. Delivered is kept in the entry apart from tried, so a restart does not repeat a notice
that arrived and a failed one is not taken for one: both refused, it tries again CC_ROOM_NOTICE_RETRY seconds (300)
later, doubling up to 6 h, NOTICE_TRIES times at most; after the last refused try it says so once in its repo's
channel (a plain `cc-notify --major`), and the launch keeps waiting.

PRIORITY (2026-10-03: the owner said "bump it up in priority" about a worker sixth in the queue behind five
launches all waiting on load, and nothing could reorder it: the waiter's own path is its entry's name, so renaming
the file breaks it). `bump` stamps `bumped` (the time) in the launch's entry, waiting or lost; the FIFO puts bumped
entries first, the latest bump first, then the rest oldest first. Being first still waits for room. `now` stamps
`now`, and its waiter takes a slot and starts on its next poll whatever the room says, cap, pace and load
included; it is a person's ask, so nothing sets it on its own. Both stamps live in the entry on disk, the waiter
reads them back under the lock on every poll before it writes the entry again, and a resumed entry keeps them.

HEAVY (2026-09-27: place_anneal ran at 4% CPU at load 160 beside ~20 sessions). `heavy` is FIFO over CC_HEAVY_CAP
slots (cc-config, default a third of the cores, at least 1) and waits on nothing else: the load is the sessions',
so waiting for it to fall would never end. The slot taken, CMD runs in a transient `systemd-run --user --scope`
directly in app.slice with CPUWeight=CC_HEAVY_CPU_WEIGHT (1000). A weight only counts against siblings, and the
sessions' tmux-spawn scopes sit directly in app.slice, so the run gets ten sessions' share of their CPU; the cap
bounds how many such runs there are. lander.slice, a peer of app.slice, keeps the share landings had. Nothing
system-level: a user scope, no unit file. With no user bus (or CC_HEAVY_SCOPE=0) it runs unscoped and says so in
one line. Heavy runs take no worker slot.

TRICKLE (2026-10-01, after the 18:32Z restart: load 22-30 on 6 cores from landings, git, lualatex, ffmpeg and ~63
sessions coming back, not from the 7 workers running, held 14 launches for over an hour, two of them the owner had
put first; again on the tower 2026-10-12, load 40-50 on 20 cores held 15 for about four hours). Load nobody can stop
would hold the queue for as long as it lasts. So while the box is overloaded() by its load average alone, with no
cpu or memory pressure (the lander's loaded()), the oldest launch still starts once no worker has started here for
CC_ROOM_TRICKLE seconds (cc-config, 600; 0 turns it off). Pressure still holds every launch, and the lander's own
overloaded() is unchanged. The last start here is the mtime of `started` in CC_ROOM_DIR; none yet counts as long ago.
Env (tests): CC_ROOM_TMUX the tmux binary · CC_ROOM_BIN where cc-broker and cc-notify are.
"""
# recurring-defect-ok: pause-hold-missing — cc refuses --go on a paused repo before admit, and cc-loop checks cc-pause as its first act
import fcntl
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(BIN))   # core/, for the lander package — not core/lander, whose types.py would shadow
try:
    from lander import admission  # noqa: E402
except Exception as e:   # a broken lander is a crash (2), never "queued" (1): cc fails open on anything but 0 and 1
    print(f"cc-room: {type(e).__name__}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)
WINDOW = 5 * 3600


def room_dir():
    return os.environ.get("CC_ROOM_DIR") or f"{HOME}/.cc/state/room"


def cfg(key, default):
    try:
        out = subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-config", "get", key, str(default)], capture_output=True, text=True,
                             timeout=10).stdout.strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        out = os.environ.get(key, "")
    try:
        return int(out)
    except ValueError:
        return default


def starttime(pid):
    """The kernel's start time of pid, or None when it is gone — a pid alone can be reused."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            return f.read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[19]
    except (OSError, IndexError):
        return None


LOST_MAX = 7 * 86400
# what a relaunch needs again, and nothing more: the keys `cc … --go` carries into the window (its `pre` list, which
# the selfcheck holds this to), CC_CLAUDE it sets in front of the loop, and the knobs cc-room itself reads. An
# allow-list, so a token that rides in the environment is never written into an entry or onto tmux's argv.
RELAUNCH_ENV = ("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", "CC_NOTIFY_LOG", "CC_LIMIT_STAMP", "CC_FAILURES", "CC_WORKER_SANDBOX",
                "CC_EGRESS_ALLOW", "CC_WORKER_MODEL", "CC_LOOP_TRIM", "CC_SPEND_TIER", "CC_ROOM_DIR", "CC_WORKER_CAP",
                "CC_WORKER_PACE_MARGIN", "LANDER_LOADAVG", "LANDER_PSI_DIR", "CC_LAND", "CC_SLACK_ALIAS", "CC_ORCH_REPO", "CC_DISPATCH_WIN",
                "CC_CLAUDE", "CC_CONFIG", "CC_STATUSLINE_DIR", "CC_ROOM_POLL", "CC_ROOM_NOTICE_AFTER", "CC_ROOM_NOTICE_RETRY",
                "CC_ROOM_GRACE", "CC_ROOM_BIN", "CC_ROOM_TMUX", "CC_ROOM_TRICKLE",
                # a worker the hub placed here: resumed after a restart, its board, notices and DONE still go home
                "CC_PEER_SPOOL", "CC_PEER_REPO", "CC_PEER_TRACK")
NOTICE_TRIES = 8
SHELLS = ("sh", "bash", "dash", "zsh")
MARKS = ("bumped", "now")   # a person's priority stamps (PRIORITY): only `bump` and `now` write them


def read_entry(p):
    """an entry as a dict: JSON, or the older `pid start what` line; {} when unreadable"""
    try:
        raw = open(p).read()
    except OSError:
        return {}
    try:
        e = json.loads(raw)
        if isinstance(e, dict):
            return e
    except ValueError:
        pass
    try:
        pid, st, what = raw.split(" ", 2)
        return {"pid": int(pid), "start": st, "what": what.strip()}
    except ValueError:
        return {}


def alive(e):
    return bool(e.get("pid")) and starttime(e["pid"]) == e.get("start")


def lost(e):
    """the waiter died with its tmux server — a restart, not a kill — and the entry can start it again"""
    # the brief: "a launch waiting in cc-room survives a user-service restart" — a server gone is that restart
    srv = e.get("server") or []
    return (len(srv) == 2 and starttime(srv[0]) != srv[1] and bool(e.get("cmd"))
            and time.time() - float(e.get("queued") or 0) < LOST_MAX)


def server_up(e):
    srv = e.get("server") or []
    return len(srv) == 2 and starttime(srv[0]) == srv[1]


def rewrite(p, e):
    """an entry written again in place, 0600; the tmp is this process's own, so a concurrent writer's is never torn"""
    tmp = f"{p}.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
    with os.fdopen(os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600), "w") as f:
        json.dump(e, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)


def settling(p, e):
    """a dead waiter whose tmux server is still up: a kill, or a restart whose server has not finished stopping (it
    ends the waiter first). True while CC_ROOM_GRACE runs from the first sighting, stamped in the entry. Callers hold
    the room lock, and the stamp goes on the entry as it is on disk now: one cancelled since e was read stays gone"""
    if not (e.get("cmd") and server_up(e)):
        return False
    seen = float(e.get("dead_seen") or 0)
    if not seen:
        cur = read_entry(p)
        if cur.get("pid") != e.get("pid") or cur.get("start") != e.get("start"):
            return bool(cur)   # gone (a cancel): nothing to stamp; written again by its waiter: not ours to stamp
        cur["dead_seen"] = e["dead_seen"] = time.time()
        try:
            rewrite(p, cur)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return True
    return time.time() - seen < float(os.environ.get("CC_ROOM_GRACE") or 300)


def live(sub):
    """{path: (pid, what)} of the entries in sub/ whose process is still the one that wrote them; a lost queue entry,
    or one still settling, stays on disk but out of the answer, and the rest go."""
    out = {}
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(f"{room_dir()}/{sub}/*")):
        if p.endswith(".tmp"):   # another process's entry, half written
            continue
        e = read_entry(p)
        if e and alive(e):
            out[p] = (int(e["pid"]), e.get("what", ""))
        elif not (sub == "queue" and e and (lost(e) or settling(p, e))):
            try:
                os.unlink(p)
            except OSError:
                pass
    return out


def ordered(sub):
    """the live entries of sub/ in the order they go: bumped first, the latest bump first, then oldest first"""
    return sorted(live(sub), key=lambda p: (-float(read_entry(p).get("bumped") or 0), p))


def lost_entries():
    """{path: entry} of the queue entries a restart left behind"""
    live("queue")   # clears the dead ones first
    return {p: e for p in sorted(glob.glob(f"{room_dir()}/queue/*")) if not p.endswith(".tmp")
            for e in [read_entry(p)] if e and not alive(e) and lost(e)}


def five_hour(now):
    """(used%, resets_at) from cc-usage-poll's now.json while it is under CC_USAGE_FRESH (180) s old, else from the
    freshest statusline file; either only while its five-hour window is still open, else None."""
    try:
        u = json.load(open(f"{os.environ.get('CC_USAGE_DIR') or HOME + '/.cc/state/usage'}/now.json"))
        used, resets = float(u["five_hour"]["used_percentage"]), float(u["five_hour"]["resets_at"])
        if now - float(u["at"]) < float(os.environ.get("CC_USAGE_FRESH") or 180) and resets > now:
            return used, resets
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        pass   # no poller, or a reading it has not made: the statusline files, as before it existed
    d = os.environ.get("CC_STATUSLINE_DIR") or f"{HOME}/.cc/state/statusline"
    files = sorted(glob.glob(f"{d}/*.json"), key=lambda p: os.path.getmtime(p) if os.path.exists(p) else 0,
                   reverse=True)
    for p in files[:200]:
        try:
            fh = json.load(open(p))["raw"]["rate_limits"]["five_hour"]
            used, resets = float(fh["used_percentage"]), float(fh["resets_at"])
        except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
            continue
        if resets > now:
            return used, resets
    return None


PEER_REST = 600
PEER_LAUNCH_TIMEOUT = 330


def peer_dir():
    return os.environ.get("CC_PEER_DIR") or f"{HOME}/.cc/peer"
_peer_rest = [0.0]   # until when the peer is not asked again, after a launch it did not take


def why_not(now=None):
    """"" when there is room for one more worker on this host, else the one-line reason."""
    return pace_why(now) or host_why()


def host_why():
    """"" when this host's own cap and load leave room, else why not."""
    cap = cfg("CC_WORKER_CAP", 12)
    held = len(live("slots"))
    if held >= cap:
        return f"{held} worker loops are running and the cap is {cap} (CC_WORKER_CAP)"
    busy = admission.overloaded()
    if busy and not trickle_due():
        return f"the box is busy: {busy}; landings go first"
    return ""


def trickle_due():
    """True when a hold by the load average alone may let one launch through: no cpu or memory pressure (loaded()),
    and no worker has started here for CC_ROOM_TRICKLE seconds (TRICKLE in the doc; 0 turns it off)"""
    # the brief: "the load came from landing gates, git, lualatex, ffmpeg and session churn ... nothing a seat could
    # stop would make room" — so the load average alone never holds forever; pressure (loaded()) still does
    every = cfg("CC_ROOM_TRICKLE", 600)
    if every <= 0 or admission.loaded():
        return False
    try:
        return time.time() - os.stat(f"{room_dir()}/started").st_mtime >= every
    except OSError:
        return True   # no worker has started here yet


def peer_why():
    """"" when the peer says it has room, else why not — no peer configured is a reason too."""
    if not os.path.exists(f"{peer_dir()}/dest"):
        return "no peer is configured"   # most hosts: asked without a process
    if time.time() < _peer_rest[0]:
        return "the peer did not take the last launch; it is asked again later"
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-peer", "room"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        return f"cc-peer room failed ({type(e).__name__})"
    return "" if p.returncode == 0 else (p.stdout.strip().splitlines() or ["the peer has no room"])[0]


def place(now=None, movable=True):
    """-> (where, why): ("here", "") with room on this host, ("peer", "") with room on the peer only, else ("", the
    reason). The brief: "starts the worker on the hub's host if that host has room, else on the peer, else queues it"."""
    pace = pace_why(now)
    if pace:
        return "", pace   # the account's, and the peer runs on the same account
    here = host_why()
    if not here:
        return "here", ""
    if not movable:
        return "", here
    there = peer_why()
    return ("peer", "") if not there else ("", f"{here}; and {there}")


def pace_why(now=None):
    """"" while the five-hour usage is not running ahead of its window, else why."""
    now = now or time.time()
    fh = five_hour(now)
    if fh:
        used, resets = fh
        margin = cfg("CC_WORKER_PACE_MARGIN", 10)
        elapsed = max(0.0, min(100.0, 100.0 * (WINDOW - (resets - now)) / WINDOW))
        # the brief: "five-hour usage not running ahead of the window's elapsed time, beyond a margin"
        if used > elapsed + margin:
            return (f"five-hour usage is at {used:.0f}% with {elapsed:.0f}% of the window gone, more than "
                    f"{margin} points ahead (CC_WORKER_PACE_MARGIN); it resets at "
                    f"{time.strftime('%H:%M', time.localtime(resets))}")
    return ""


def write_entry(sub, name, what, extra=None):
    """this process's entry: `pid start what` for a slot, JSON with the launch's own record for a queue entry"""
    os.makedirs(f"{room_dir()}/{sub}", exist_ok=True)
    p = f"{room_dir()}/{sub}/{name}"
    tmp = f"{p}.tmp"
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)   # a queue entry carries the launch's environment
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
        if extra is None:
            f.write(f"{os.getpid()} {starttime(os.getpid())} {what}\n")
        else:
            json.dump({**extra, "pid": os.getpid(), "start": starttime(os.getpid()), "what": what}, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    return p


def logline(path, msg):
    line = f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime())} {msg}\n"
    sys.stderr.write(line)
    if path:
        try:
            with open(path, "a") as f:
                f.write(line)
        except OSError:
            pass


class Lock:
    def __enter__(self):
        self.f = open(f"{room_dir()}/.lock", "w")
        fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *a):
        self.f.close()


def tmux(e=None):
    """the tmux argv, pointed at the server entry e waits in when it saved one; an older entry has no socket and
    gets the default server, as before"""
    sock = (e or {}).get("socket")
    return [os.environ.get("CC_ROOM_TMUX") or "tmux"] + (["-S", sock] if sock else [])


def where():
    """(server [pid, start], session, window, socket) of the tmux pane this runs in, all None outside tmux"""
    t = os.environ.get("TMUX", "").split(",")
    if len(t) < 2 or not t[1].isdigit() or not starttime(t[1]):
        return None, None, None, None
    sess, win = "main", None
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(tmux({"socket": t[0]}) + ["display-message", "-p", "-t", os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE", ""),   # $TMUX's server
                            "#{session_name}\t#{window_name}"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        if r.returncode == 0 and "\t" in r.stdout:
            sess, win = r.stdout.rstrip("\n").split("\t", 1)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    return [t[1], starttime(t[1])], sess, win, t[0] or None


def admit(what, cmd, log, resume=None):
    """Wait for room, then exec cmd here, or end once the peer has taken it. FAIL OPEN: any error on the way (a room
    dir it cannot write, a lock, a bad reading) runs cmd here anyway with one line saying admission failed — a crash
    here would otherwise leave a queued track that never runs and a window nobody reads. A cancelled launch, and one
    the peer took, are the two ways out without cmd.
    Failing open drops the queue entry first: CMD runs under this same pid, so an entry it left would read as a
    waiter still queued at the head of the FIFO, and a resumed one as a launch `resume` should start again."""
    mine = {}
    try:
        if wait_for_room(what, cmd, log, resume, mine=mine) != "here":   # cancelled, or running on the peer
            sys.exit(0)
    except Exception as e:   # noqa: BLE001 — every failure here is the same one: no admission, so start anyway
        logline(log, f"cc-room: admission failed for {what} ({type(e).__name__}: {e}); starting it anyway")
        forget([mine.get("entry"), resume and f"{room_dir()}/queue/{os.path.basename(resume)}"], what, log)
    os.execvp(cmd[0], cmd)


def forget(paths, what, log):
    """unlink these queue entries and their tombstones, without the lock (it may be what failed): the launch is
    about to run unadmitted, and nothing may later take it for one still waiting, or one a restart left behind"""
    for p in filter(None, paths):
        for x in (p, f"{room_dir()}/cancelled/{os.path.basename(p)}"):
            try:
                os.unlink(x)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                pass
            except OSError as e:
                logline(log, f"cc-room: could not drop {x} for {what} ({e}); `resume` will find this window "
                             f"running something other than its waiter and leave it alone")


def wait_for_room(what, cmd, log, resume=None, kind="worker", mine=None):
    """-> "here" holding a slot here, "peer" once the peer has started it, False cancelled. kind "worker" waits on
    place() in queue/, "heavy" on the heavy cap in heavy-queue/; only a worker's entry is kept through a restart, only
    a worker's wait gives notice and only a worker's cc-loop launch can go to the peer.
    mine["entry"] is the queue entry once written, for a caller that fails open to drop."""
    mine = {} if mine is None else mine
    qsub, ssub = ("queue", "slots") if kind == "worker" else ("heavy-queue", "heavy")
    os.makedirs(room_dir(), exist_ok=True)
    bound = cfg("CC_ROOM_NOTICE_AFTER", 3600)
    with Lock():
        if resume:   # relaunched by `resume`: the same entry, so the same place in the queue
            name = os.path.basename(resume)
            if not os.path.exists(f"{room_dir()}/{qsub}/{name}"):
                logline(log, f"cc-room: {what} was cancelled while it was down; not starting it")
                return False
            if alive(read_entry(f"{room_dir()}/{qsub}/{name}")):   # a fresh `cc … --go` adopted it first
                logline(log, f"cc-room: {what} is already waiting in another window; not starting it twice")
                return False
        else:        # a fresh `cc … --go` of a track a restart left queued takes over its place
            name = next((os.path.basename(p) for p, e in (lost_entries() if kind == "worker" else {}).items()
                         if e.get("what") == what), None) or f"{time.time_ns()}-{os.getpid()}"
        old = read_entry(f"{room_dir()}/{qsub}/{name}")
        rec = {"queued": old.get("queued") or time.time(), "noticed": bool(old.get("noticed")), "cmd": cmd,
               "cwd": os.getcwd(), "log": log, "notice_tries": int(old.get("notice_tries") or 0),
               "notice_next": float(old.get("notice_next") or 0), **{k: old[k] for k in MARKS if k in old}}
        if kind == "worker":
            server, sess, win, sock = where()
            alias = os.environ.get("CC_SLACK_ALIAS", "")
            repo = what.split("/", 1)[0]
            rec.update(server=server, socket=sock, session=sess, window=win or what,
                       env={k: os.environ[k] for k in RELAUNCH_ENV if k in os.environ},
                       target=old.get("target") or (f"{repo}@{alias}" if alias else repo))
        me = mine["entry"] = write_entry(qsub, name, what, rec)
    poll = float(os.environ.get("CC_ROOM_POLL") or 60)
    movable = kind == "worker" and bool(cmd) and os.path.basename(cmd[0]) == "cc-loop"   # a member's cc-sandbox launch stays home
    retry = float(os.environ.get("CC_ROOM_NOTICE_RETRY") or 300)
    said, last = None, 0.0
    while True:
        tell_now = ask_peer = False
        with Lock():
            if not os.path.exists(me):
                tomb = f"{room_dir()}/cancelled/{name}"
                if os.path.exists(tomb):
                    os.unlink(tomb)
                    logline(log, f"cc-room: {what} was cancelled from the queue; not starting it")
                    return False
                # gone with no tombstone: a waiter still running the older code read this JSON entry as a dead
                # one and removed it (the deploy window) — write it again rather than take it for a cancel
                write_entry(qsub, name, what, rec)
            cur = read_entry(me)   # a `bump` or `now` since the last poll: kept in every write from here on
            rec.update({k: cur[k] for k in MARKS if k in cur})
            waiting = ordered(qsub)
            first = (waiting or [me])[0] == me   # FIFO, bumped first: the launch at the head goes first
            if rec.get("now"):   # a person said start it now (PRIORITY): no room, pace or turn is asked
                write_entry(ssub, str(os.getpid()), what)
                os.unlink(me)
                logline(log, f"cc-room: {what} starts now, as asked with `cc-room now`, room or not")
                return "here"
            if kind == "worker":   # this host here, under the lock; the peer (TWO HOSTS) below, once it is released
                pace = pace_why() if first else ""
                why = (pace or host_why()) if first else ""
                ask_peer = bool(first and why and not pace and movable)
            else:
                why = heavy_why() if first else ""
            spot = "here" if first and not why else ""   # `spot`, since where() is the pane's
            if spot == "here":
                write_entry(ssub, str(os.getpid()), what)
                if kind == "worker":   # TRICKLE counts from the last worker started here
                    open(f"{room_dir()}/started", "w").close()
                os.unlink(me)
                if said is not None:
                    logline(log, f"cc-room: room now — {what} starts")
                return "here"
            if not first:
                ahead = waiting.index(me) if me in waiting else 0
                why = f"{ahead} launch(es) queued ahead of it"
            # the brief: "tells the seat that dispatched it when it has waited past a bound" — once delivered, kept in
            # the entry; a try is stamped before it is made, so a restart mid-try waits out its backoff too
            if (kind == "worker" and not rec["noticed"] and time.time() - rec["queued"] >= bound
                    and rec["notice_tries"] < NOTICE_TRIES and time.time() >= rec["notice_next"]):
                rec["notice_tries"] += 1
                rec["notice_next"] = time.time() + min(retry * 2 ** (rec["notice_tries"] - 1), 6 * 3600)
                tell_now = True
                write_entry(qsub, name, what, rec)
        if ask_peer:   # never under the lock: a peer slow to answer would hold up every admit, heavy run, cancel and resume
            got = to_peer(what, cmd, log, me, name)
            if got == "peer":
                return "peer"
            if got == "cancelled":
                continue   # the loop's top reads the tombstone
            why = f"{why}; and {got}"
        if tell_now:
            if notice(what, rec, why, log):
                rec["noticed"] = True
            elif rec["notice_tries"] >= NOTICE_TRIES and not rec.get("unheard_said"):
                rec["unheard_said"] = unheard(what, rec, why, log)
            with Lock():
                if os.path.exists(me):   # a cancel in between moved it away: never write it back
                    cur = read_entry(me)   # nor drop a bump or now made while the notice went out
                    rec.update({k: cur[k] for k in MARKS if k in cur})
                    write_entry(qsub, name, what, rec)
        # one line when the reason changes, and one an hour while it holds, so a long wait is seen and not re-logged
        if why != said or time.time() - last >= 3600:
            logline(log, f"cc-room: {what} queued — {why}")
            said, last = why, time.time()
        time.sleep(poll)


def to_peer(what, cmd, log, me, name):
    """Hand the launch at the head of the queue to the peer, with the room lock NOT held: -> "peer" when the peer
    started it (its entry dropped), "cancelled" when a cancel came first, else why it still waits here. A peer that
    answered no, or not at all, is not asked again for PEER_REST seconds."""
    there = peer_why()
    if there:
        if os.path.exists(f"{peer_dir()}/dest") and time.time() >= _peer_rest[0]:
            _peer_rest[0] = time.time() + PEER_REST   # it said no or did not answer: a rest, not a 40 s ask a poll
        return there
    with Lock():
        if not os.path.exists(me):
            return "cancelled"
        e = read_entry(me)
        e["placing"] = time.time()   # PLACING, in the header: a waiter killed from here on is never relaunched blind
        rewrite(me, e)
    try:   # cc-peer's own three tries take at most about 280 s; this bounds a call that never returns
        rc = subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-peer", "launch", what] + (["--log", log] if log else []) + ["--"] + list(cmd),
                            timeout=PEER_LAUNCH_TIMEOUT).returncode
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        logline(log, f"cc-room: cc-peer launch for {what} failed ({type(e).__name__})")
        rc = 1
    if rc:
        _peer_rest[0] = time.time() + PEER_REST
        with Lock():
            e = read_entry(me)
            if e.pop("placing", None):   # it waits here again, so a restart may relaunch it as before
                rewrite(me, e)
        return "the peer did not take it (cc-peer launch above); it waits here"
    with Lock():   # started there: whatever came in meanwhile, the entry goes, so nothing runs it here too
        tomb = f"{room_dir()}/cancelled/{name}"
        if os.path.exists(tomb):
            logline(log, f"cc-room: {what} was cancelled while the peer was starting it; it runs on the peer")
        for x in (me, tomb):
            try:
                os.unlink(x)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                pass
    return "peer"


def ccboard():
    """cc-board imported as a module beside THIS copy, for the fixture rule: asked, never copied (its selfcheck's tripwire)"""
    if not hasattr(ccboard, "mod"):
        import importlib.machinery
        import importlib.util
        s = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("ccboard", importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("ccboard", f"{BIN}/cc-board"))
        ccboard.mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(s)
        s.loader.exec_module(ccboard.mod)
    return ccboard.mod


def test_namespace(what):
    """a launch under a suite's scratch prefix (cc-board FIXTURE): it has no seat to tell. A cc-board that will not load
    is not a reason to stay quiet, so the launch is then taken as real and its seat is told."""
    try:
        return what.startswith(ccboard().FIXTURE)
    except Exception:
        return False


def notice(what, rec, why, log):
    """-> True once someone has it: the broker took it, or cc-notify exited 0 (a test namespace has nobody to tell)"""
    mins = int((time.time() - rec["queued"]) // 60)
    line = (f"{what} has waited {mins // 60} h {mins % 60:02d} min in the worker queue and has not started: {why}. "
            f"It starts on its own when there is room; `cc-room cancel {what}` drops it, "
            f"`cc-room bump {what}` moves it to the head of the queue and `cc-room now {what}` starts it now.")
    if test_namespace(what):   # a suite's scratch namespace has no seat, and must not reach the box's broker
        logline(log, f"cc-room: told nobody (a test namespace): {line}")
        return True
    b = os.environ.get("CC_ROOM_BIN") or BIN
    how = ""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["timeout", "30", f"{b}/cc-broker", "inject", "--no-start", "--source", "room",
                            rec["target"], line], capture_output=True, timeout=40)
        how = rec["target"] if r.returncode == 0 else ""
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    if not how:
        stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%MZ", time.gmtime(rec["queued"]))
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([f"{b}/cc-notify", "--ask", "--id", f"room:{what}:{stamp}", "-t", f"{what} queued",
                                line], capture_output=True, timeout=60)
            how = "a planner request (cc-notify --ask)" if r.returncode == 0 else ""
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            pass
    if how:
        logline(log, f"cc-room: told {how}: {line}")
        return True
    logline(log, f"cc-room: could not tell anyone that {what} waits (the broker and cc-notify both refused; "
                 f"try {rec.get('notice_tries', 1)} of {NOTICE_TRIES})")
    return False


def unheard(what, rec, why, log):
    """NOTICE_TRIES refused notices: say so once in the dispatcher's repo channel (a plain --major cc-notify, a door
    apart from the broker and the request file) rather than only in the log. -> True once that post was taken; the
    --id keeps a restart from saying it twice. The launch keeps waiting either way: nothing is dropped."""
    logline(log, f"cc-room: nobody took the notice that {what} waits, after {NOTICE_TRIES} tries; saying so in its channel")
    mins = int((time.time() - rec["queued"]) // 60)
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%MZ", time.gmtime(rec["queued"]))
    line = (f"{what} has waited {mins // 60} h {mins % 60:02d} min in the worker queue: {why}. Its seat "
            f"({rec.get('target', '?')}) did not take the notice after {NOTICE_TRIES} tries. It starts on its own "
            f"when there is room; `cc-room cancel {what}` drops it.")
    b = os.environ.get("CC_ROOM_BIN") or BIN
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([f"{b}/cc-notify", "--major", "--id", f"room-unheard:{what}:{stamp}",
                            "-t", f"{what} queued", line], capture_output=True, timeout=60)
        if r.returncode == 0:
            logline(log, f"cc-room: said in the channel: {line}")
            return True
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    logline(log, f"cc-room: could not say in the channel that {what} waits either")
    return False


def proc_argv(pid):
    """argv of pid, [] for a zombie or a kernel thread, None when it cannot be read"""
    try:
        return open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode(errors="replace").split("\0")[:-1]
    except OSError:
        return None


def tree(pid):
    """pid and every process below it"""
    kids = {}
    for s in glob.glob("/proc/[0-9]*/stat"):
        try:
            kids.setdefault(int(open(s).read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[1]), []).append(int(s.split("/")[2]))
        except (OSError, IndexError, ValueError):
            continue
    out, todo = [], [int(pid)]
    while todo:
        x = todo.pop()
        if x not in out:
            out.append(x)
            todo += kids.get(x, [])
    return out


def waiter_argv(a, name):
    """a is the `cc-room admit --resume <name>` waiter `resume` started, the shell line around it, or the idle
    shell that line ends in — and nothing it went on to run"""
    if not a:
        return False
    sh = os.path.basename(a[0]).lstrip("-")
    if sh in SHELLS and len(a) == 1:
        return True
    if sh in SHELLS and len(a) == 3 and a[1] == "-c":
        try:
            a = shlex.split(a[2])
        except ValueError:
            return False
    i = next((i for i, x in enumerate(a) if os.path.basename(x) == "cc-room"), None)
    return i is not None and a[i + 1:i + 4] == ["admit", "--resume", name]


def window_state(tm, wid, name):
    """what is in the window an earlier resume opened: "gone"; "waiter" when every process in it is its waiter
    (waiter_argv); "running" when anything else is (the launch it ran, a person's command); None when unsure"""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(tm + ["list-windows", "-a", "-F", "#{window_id}"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=10)
        if r.returncode:
            return None
        if wid not in r.stdout.split():
            return "gone"
        r = subprocess.run(tm + ["list-panes", "-t", wid, "-F", "#{pane_pid}"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    pids = [x for x in r.stdout.split() if x.isdigit()]
    if r.returncode or not pids:
        return None
    for pid in (x for pp in pids for x in tree(pp)):
        a = proc_argv(pid)
        if a is None and starttime(pid) is None:
            continue   # it ended while we looked
        if not a:
            return None
        if not waiter_argv(a, name):
            return "running"
    return "waiter"


def resume():
    """relaunch each lost entry in a new window of its tmux session; -> how many. One resume at a time; the tmux
    calls run outside the room lock, and every change to an entry re-reads it under the lock first"""
    n = 0
    os.makedirs(room_dir(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(f"{room_dir()}/.resume.lock", "w") as one:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(one, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            print("another cc-room resume is running")
            return 0
        with Lock():
            todo = {p: e for p, e in lost_entries().items()
                    if time.time() - float(e.get("relaunched") or 0) >= 600}   # else its admit has not written yet
        for p, e in todo.items():
            n += resume_one(p, e)
    return n


def resume_one(p, e):
    what, sess = e.get("what", ""), e.get("session") or "main"
    win, name, tm = e.get("window") or what, os.path.basename(p), tmux(e)

    def drop(why):
        with Lock():
            if read_entry(p) == e:   # still the entry read, untouched by a cancel, an adoption or its own admit
                os.unlink(p)
        logline(e.get("log"), f"cc-room: dropped {what} from the queue: {why}")
        print(f"dropped {what}: {why}")
        return 0

    # the brief: "have resume check the peer (or skip) for an entry left in 'placing' and not relaunch blindly"
    if e.get("placing"):   # PLACING: its waiter died while it handed this launch to the peer
        repo, _, track = what.partition("/")
        try:
            rec = open(f"{peer_dir()}/placed/{repo}/{track}").read().split()
        except OSError:
            rec = []
        # cc-peer stamps whole seconds, so the stamp is compared floored; the same second counts as this launch
        if len(rec) >= 3 and rec[2].isdigit() and int(rec[2]) >= int(float(e["placing"])):
            return drop(f"it was started on {rec[1]} before the restart and runs there")
        if not e.get("placing_said"):
            with Lock():
                if read_entry(p) == e:
                    e["placing_said"] = time.time()
                    rewrite(p, e)
            logline(e.get("log"), f"cc-room: {what} not relaunched: its waiter died while handing it to the peer, "
                                  f"which may be running it; check the peer, then `cc-room cancel {what}` or "
                                  f"launch it again")
        print(f"left {what}: it may already run on the peer")
        return 0
    try:
        if subprocess.run(tm + ["has-session", "-t", sess], capture_output=True, timeout=10).returncode:
            return 0   # its server is not back yet: the next tick tries again
        opened = e.get("opened") or []
        if len(opened) == 3 and opened[2] and starttime(opened[1]) == opened[2]:
            # the window an earlier resume opened on this same server (a server never reuses a window id), and its
            # admit never wrote the entry. Closed only while it holds nothing that started: see the header
            state = window_state(tm, opened[0], name)
            if state == "running":   # the admit failed open and ran the launch; never a second one, never a kill
                return drop(f"its launch already runs in window {opened[0]}, which is left alone")
            if state is None:
                logline(e.get("log"), f"cc-room: {what} not relaunched: cannot tell what runs in window "
                                      f"{opened[0]}, so it and the queue entry are left alone")
                print(f"left {what}: cannot tell what runs in window {opened[0]}")
                return 0
            if state == "waiter":
                with Lock():
                    if read_entry(p) != e:
                        return 0   # its admit wrote after all, or a cancel or an adoption came first
                    subprocess.run(tm + ["kill-window", "-t", opened[0]], capture_output=True, timeout=10)
        wins = subprocess.run(tm + ["list-windows", "-t", sess, "-F", "#{window_name}"],
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10).stdout.splitlines()
        if win in wins:
            return drop(f"a window {win} is already open in {sess}")
        # the environment rides on -e, not in the shell line: the line stays in the pane's argv all wait
        env = [x for k, v in sorted((e.get("env") or {}).items()) if k in RELAUNCH_ENV
               for x in ("-e", f"{k}={v}")]
        line = (f"{shlex.join([f'{BIN}/cc-room', 'admit', '--resume', name] + (['--log', e['log']] if e.get('log') else []) + [what, '--'] + e['cmd'])}"
                f"; echo; echo {shlex.quote('[worker finished — shell stays]')}; exec bash")
        cwd = e.get("cwd") if os.path.isdir(e.get("cwd") or "") else HOME
        r = subprocess.run(tm + ["new-window", "-d", "-P", "-F", "#{window_id} #{pid}", "-t", sess, "-n", win,
                                 "-c", cwd] + env + [line], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as x:
        print(f"could not relaunch {what}: {x}")
        return 0
    if r.returncode:
        print(f"could not relaunch {what}: {r.stderr.strip()}")
        return 0
    wid, _, spid = r.stdout.strip().partition(" ")
    with Lock():
        cur = read_entry(p)
        if cur == e:
            e["relaunched"] = time.time()
            e["opened"] = [wid, spid, starttime(spid)] if wid and spid.isdigit() else []
            rewrite(p, e)
        elif not alive(cur):   # cancelled or adopted while the window opened: its admit finds that and starts nothing
            print(f"relaunched {what}, but its entry changed meanwhile; the new window starts nothing")
            return 0
        # else its admit already took the entry back: it is waiting again
    logline(e.get("log"), f"cc-room: {what} relaunched after a restart, still queued in its old place")
    print(f"resumed {what} in {sess}:{win}")
    return 1


def cancel(what):
    """drop every queued entry of that launch, waiting or lost; -> how many. A waiting one moves to cancelled/,
    the tombstone its waiter reads as the cancel and removes; a lost one has no waiter and just goes."""
    n = 0
    os.makedirs(f"{room_dir()}/cancelled", exist_ok=True)
    with Lock():
        for sub in ("queue", "heavy-queue"):
            for p in glob.glob(f"{room_dir()}/{sub}/*"):
                e = read_entry(p)
                if p.endswith(".tmp") or e.get("what") != what:
                    continue
                if alive(e):
                    os.replace(p, f"{room_dir()}/cancelled/{os.path.basename(p)}")
                else:
                    os.unlink(p)
                n += 1
    return n


def mark(what, key, val):
    """stamp key=val in every queued entry of that launch, waiting or lost; -> how many"""
    n = 0
    os.makedirs(room_dir(), exist_ok=True)
    with Lock():
        for sub in ("queue", "heavy-queue"):
            for p in glob.glob(f"{room_dir()}/{sub}/*"):
                e = read_entry(p)
                if p.endswith(".tmp") or e.get("what") != what:
                    continue
                e[key] = val
                rewrite(p, e)
                n += 1
    return n


def heavy_cap():
    return max(1, cfg("CC_HEAVY_CAP", max(1, (os.cpu_count() or 1) // 3)))


def heavy_why():
    held, cap = len(live("heavy")), heavy_cap()
    return f"{held} heavy runs are going and the cap is {cap} (CC_HEAVY_CAP)" if held >= cap else ""


def heavy_argv(label, cmd, scoped):
    if not scoped:
        return cmd
    unit = f"heavy-{''.join(c if c.isalnum() else '-' for c in label)[:60]}-{os.getpid()}"
    weight = cfg("CC_HEAVY_CPU_WEIGHT", 1000)
    return ["systemd-run", "--user", "--scope", "--quiet", "--collect", "--slice=app.slice", f"--unit={unit}",
            "-p", f"CPUWeight={weight}", "--"] + cmd


def heavy(label, cmd, log):
    """a heavy slot, then exec cmd in its scope; fails open like admit"""
    scoped, mine = False, {}
    try:
        if not wait_for_room(label, cmd, log, kind="heavy", mine=mine):
            sys.exit(0)
        scoped = os.environ.get("CC_HEAVY_SCOPE", "1") != "0" and admission.scope_available()
        if not scoped:
            logline(log, f"cc-room: {label} runs without its scope (no user systemd bus, or CC_HEAVY_SCOPE=0)")
    except Exception as e:   # noqa: BLE001
        logline(log, f"cc-room: heavy admission failed for {label} ({type(e).__name__}: {e}); starting it anyway")
        forget([mine.get("entry")], label, log)   # else it heads the heavy FIFO for as long as CMD runs
    argv = heavy_argv(label, cmd, scoped)
    os.execvp(argv[0], argv)


def status():
    os.makedirs(room_dir(), exist_ok=True)
    with Lock():   # its sweep removes and stamps entries: never between a cancel and the entry it read before it
        for p, (pid, what) in live("slots").items():
            print(f"running {what} (pid {pid})")
        q = live("queue")
        for p in ordered("queue"):
            e = read_entry(p)
            note = " — starting now" if e.get("now") else " — bumped" if e.get("bumped") else ""
            print(f"queued  {q[p][1]} (pid {q[p][0]}){note}")
        for p, e in lost_entries().items():
            print(f"lost    {e.get('what')} — " + ("its waiter died while handing it to the peer; check the peer, "
                  "then cancel it or launch it again" if e.get("placing") else
                  "its waiter died with a restart; `cc-room resume` starts it again"))
        for p, (pid, what) in live("heavy").items():
            print(f"heavy   {what} (pid {pid})")
        q = live("heavy-queue")
        for p in ordered("heavy-queue"):
            print(f"heavy queued {q[p][1]} (pid {q[p][0]})")
    spot, w = place()
    print({"here": "room: yes", "peer": "room: on the peer"}.get(spot, f"room: no — {w}"))


def queued():
    """the launches that have not started and will start by themselves: waiting in the queue, or lost to a restart
    for `resume` to start again. A lost entry stamped `placing` is not here: resume never relaunches it until a person
    checks the peer (PLACING, in the header), so its row still needs someone. A heavy run is not a row's launch."""
    os.makedirs(room_dir(), exist_ok=True)
    with Lock():
        names = [what for _, what in live("queue").values()] + [e.get("what") for e in lost_entries().values()
                                                                if not e.get("placing")]
    return sorted({w for w in names if w})


def selfcheck(*a, **k):
    """The cases are core/tests/selfcheck/cc-room.py, run in THIS namespace so their stubs and `global`
    rebinds land on the tool as they did when the body was here. Defining them replaces this selfcheck()."""
    path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))), "tests", "selfcheck", "cc-room.py")
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        exec(compile(fh.read(), path, "exec"), globals())
    return globals()["selfcheck"](*a, **k)


def main(a):
    if a[:1] == ["check"]:
        where, w = place()
        if w:
            print(w)
        return 0 if where else 1
    if a[:1] == ["status"]:
        status()
        return 0
    if a[:1] == ["queued"]:
        for w in queued():
            print(w)
        return 0
    if a[:1] == ["selfcheck"]:
        return selfcheck()
    if a[:1] == ["resume"]:
        resume()
        return 0
    if a[:1] == ["cancel"] and len(a) == 2:
        n = cancel(a[1])
        print(f"cancelled {a[1]}: its waiting launch exits on its next poll" if n else f"nothing queued for {a[1]}")
        return 0 if n else 1
    if a[:1] in (["bump"], ["now"]) and len(a) == 2:
        n = mark(a[1], "bumped", time.time()) if a[0] == "bump" else mark(a[1], "now", True)
        print((f"bumped {a[1]} to the head of the queue; it still waits for room" if a[0] == "bump" else
               f"{a[1]} starts on its waiter's next poll") if n else f"nothing queued for {a[1]}")
        return 0 if n else 1
    if a[:1] in (["admit"], ["heavy"]) and "--" in a:
        i = a.index("--")
        head, cmd = a[1:i], a[i + 1:]
        opts = {}
        for flag in ("--log", "--resume", "--name"):
            if flag in head:
                j = head.index(flag)
                opts[flag] = head[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(head) else None
                head = head[:j] + head[j + 2:]
        if a[0] == "admit" and len(head) == 1 and cmd and "--name" not in opts:
            admit(head[0], cmd, opts.get("--log"), opts.get("--resume"))
        if a[0] == "heavy" and not head and cmd and "--resume" not in opts:
            heavy(opts.get("--name") or os.path.basename(cmd[0]), cmd, opts.get("--log"))
    print(__doc__.split("\n\n")[0], file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except Exception as e:   # a crash is 2, never 1: cc reads 1 as "queued" and only fails open on anything else
        print(f"cc-room: {type(e).__name__}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
